WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:05.580
Why would a sensory cell accept being slow?

2
00:00:06.500 --> 00:00:08.440
A hair cell and a rod hold the answer.

3
00:00:09.483 --> 00:00:12.583
Every sensory cell turns a stimulus into a current.

4
00:00:13.463 --> 00:00:16.903
So the first question is what sits between the stimulus and the pore.

5
00:00:17.883 --> 00:00:19.643
In a hair cell, no enzyme.

6
00:00:20.663 --> 00:00:24.943
Bending the bundle pulls on tip links, and the pull opens transduction channels

7
00:00:24.943 --> 00:00:25.943
directly.

8
00:00:26.467 --> 00:00:31.707
In turtle hair cells, these channels can activate with a time constant as short as

9
00:00:31.707 --> 00:00:32.927
about 50 microseconds.

10
00:00:34.050 --> 00:00:36.270
In a rod, there is a ladder of enzymes.

11
00:00:37.370 --> 00:00:41.310
Light activates rhodopsin, then transducin, then PDE6.

12
00:00:42.300 --> 00:00:45.460
PDE6 breaks down cyclic GMP.

13
00:00:46.220 --> 00:00:50.040
Channels close, less positive charge enters, and the rod hyperpolarizes.

14
00:00:51.150 --> 00:00:56.510
A mouse rod's response to a single photon takes of order a tenth of a second or more

15
00:00:56.510 --> 00:00:57.510
to peak.

16
00:00:57.900 --> 00:01:02.200
On one time axis, the two sit more than three orders of magnitude apart.

17
00:01:03.183 --> 00:01:07.643
But one is a channel time constant, and the other a whole cell's time to peak.

18
00:01:08.523 --> 00:01:09.523
Read the gap as scale.

19
00:01:10.517 --> 00:01:14.257
Predict. What does the rod get in return for being slow?

20
00:01:20.300 --> 00:01:23.640
Gain. Enzymes keep working while active.

21
00:01:24.600 --> 00:01:29.920
In mouse rods, one absorbed photon removes about 2,000 cyclic GMP molecules.

22
00:01:31.033 --> 00:01:34.193
That is one reason a rod can signal a single photon.

23
00:01:35.313 --> 00:01:38.393
Low dark noise and a reproducible response are needed too.

24
00:01:39.383 --> 00:01:42.883
A direct channel has no enzyme cascade before the current.

25
00:01:43.823 --> 00:01:45.803
Force acts on the channel complex itself.

26
00:01:46.683 --> 00:01:47.943
The rod's price is time.

27
00:01:48.923 --> 00:01:53.123
Molecules must meet, and enzymes must turn over, before any channel changes.

28
00:01:54.216 --> 00:01:56.276
That is the one idea of this lesson.

29
00:01:57.196 --> 00:02:01.016
What sits between stimulus and pore sets what a sensory cell can do.

30
00:02:01.950 --> 00:02:04.890
The second question is how the signal leaves the cell.

31
00:02:05.670 --> 00:02:08.250
An olfactory neuron carries it on its own axon.

32
00:02:09.183 --> 00:02:13.903
A hair cell or a type II taste cell hands it to a separate afferent fibre instead.

33
00:02:14.966 --> 00:02:20.026
Together, the two questions make a table of four boxes, and five examples fill it.

34
00:02:20.950 --> 00:02:23.010
Rods and cones do not fit neatly.

35
00:02:23.890 --> 00:02:27.450
They synapse inside the retina and send no axon to the brain.

36
00:02:28.450 --> 00:02:31.110
So ask two questions of every sensory cell.

37
00:02:31.950 --> 00:02:33.590
What sits between stimulus and pore?

38
00:02:34.490 --> 00:02:35.850
And how does the signal leave?
